      "name": "a clock that went backwards hands on the budget that arrived, and never more",
      "law": "The seq and the leash",
      "obligations": ["VIII-14", "VIII-5"],
      "derivation": "Article VIII: \"**Where a door's own two readings yield a dwell below zero, the onward budget is the arriving one** — the rule that no door widens a leash is absolute, and a door's clock is never a peer's fault.\" The clock handed to this door falls by a hundred at every reading, so the onward reading is below the arrival one and the dwell is negative. The ask arrives with four hops and five thousand milliseconds, and the ask handed onward carries three hops and **five thousand milliseconds exactly** — the budget that arrived, neither more nor less. **The pinned bytes separate the three things a kit can do here and there are only three.** A kit that subtracts the dwell as it finds it hands on 5100 and has widened a leash, which Article VIII forbids absolutely. A kit that reads a backwards clock as a fault and withholds the ask puts no bytes here at all, and has punished a peer for this door's clock — the arriving message was legal in every respect. A kit that clamps the budget to zero rather than to the arriving one also puts no bytes here, because \"the budget to zero\" is one of the two conditions under which the onward ask is not made. Only the ruling produces this envelope. **And the expectation does not depend on how many readings the kit took**: every consecutive pair of this clock differs by minus one hundred, so a door that read its clock a third time in the middle still yields a negative dwell and still hands on the arriving budget. That is deliberate — the contract refuses to ask how far a queue was drawn, so a case whose answer turned on the draw count would be asserting a freedom. **The being's own answer is not asserted**, here as in `chain`: what it returns belongs to its blueprint.",
